### Portable HTTPS and request-close boundary

The shared Darwin/FoundationNetworking delegate path admits observable headers, incoming callback data, old/new accumulation and the final body copy. Opaque Foundation/TLS/header/cache/queued callback storage cannot be preadmitted by this accumulator. Geometric-growth tests establish the reported copy-work bounds only for their specified one-byte fixtures, not a universal linear-work constant. The serial delegate queue has an explicit request service class matching the existing deadline queue; this is not a hard scheduling guarantee.

`JPIPTransport.close()` cancels and joins captured HTTP/HTTPS owners and invokes WebSocket disconnect. Terminal WebSocket reader/timeout callbacks may finish later. Generation checks prevent retired results from restoring a socket or channel path. HTTP completion means finite I/O and locked result publication, not physical thread exit; blocking DNS remains a qualification limit. HTTPS completion follows task completion, session invalidation and the serial delegate-queue tail; a separate deinitialisation observation checks eventual release. The new URLProtocol, scripted callback and loopback tests (including HTTPS harness repair 24 in `CodexR3HTTPStreamingTests.swift` `40bd33c3...`, which replaces the unavailable inherited `URLProtocol` `Sendable` claim with three private `DispatchWorkItem` boundaries while keeping all `17` test declarations, `83` expectations, `3` requires, original deadlines, queue policy, and suite concurrency) remain harness coverage: they do not establish real TLS behaviour, every higher-level client interleaving, or independent JPIP interoperability. Failed 09–14, 17–18, and 22 receipts and their diagnostic observer limitations are retained. Exceptional WebSocket fixture cleanup uses a bounded completion observation with suppressed secondary failure, not an unconditional join guarantee.

